# Start and Address Agents

This tutorial starts a Jido instance, runs a ticket Agent inside it, and reaches that Agent by PID, ID, partition, stable Address, and OTP name.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "release/v3"}
])
```

## Define an Agent

The Agent is a plain definition. It has no process until you start it.

```elixir
defmodule StartGuide.TicketDesk do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "ticket_desk"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{
             opened: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.string()) |> Jido.Joi.default([])
           })
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/support/desk"

    route "ticket.open", as: :open do
      action %{subject: subject},
        schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{subject: Jido.Joi.string()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | opened: context.agent_state.opened ++ [subject]}}
      end
    end
  end
end

StartGuide.TicketDesk.open_signal!(%{subject: "Login fails"}).type
# The cell returns "ticket.open"
```

## Start an instance

A Jido instance is a named supervisor. It owns the Agent registry, the Agent supervisor, and the Runner that executes Turns. Give it a `:namespace` and persistent storage when you want durable Agents with stable Addresses.

```elixir
store_path =
  Path.join(
    System.tmp_dir!(),
    "jido-start-guide-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"
  )

{:ok, _instance} =
  Jido.start(
    name: StartGuide.Jido,
    namespace: "guides/support",
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.File, path: store_path}
  )
```

`Jido.start/1` suits scripts and Livebook. An application defines a module and adds it to its supervision tree:

<!-- partial -->
```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Jido do
  use Jido, otp_app: :my_app, namespace: "my-app/primary"
end

children = [MyApp.Jido]
```

See [Instances and Tenants](../operations/configuration-and-tenant-isolation.md) for instance options.

## Start an Agent

`Jido.start_agent/3` accepts an Agent module with `:id` and `:state`, or a complete Agent instance. It returns the PID of the AgentServer worker.

```elixir
{:ok, desk} =
  Jido.start_agent(StartGuide.Jido, StartGuide.TicketDesk,
    id: "desk-1",
    state: %{opened: ["Welcome"]}
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(desk)

instance = StartGuide.TicketDesk.instantiate!(id: "desk-2")
{:ok, second_desk} = Jido.start_agent(StartGuide.Jido, instance)

Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(desk).agent.state
# The cell returns %{opened: ["Welcome"]}
```

`Jido.AgentServer.await_ready/2` waits for Plugin startup and any persistence restore. Call it before you depend on the Agent. Without an `:id`, Jido generates one. A complete instance keeps its own ID and state, so it rejects a `:state` option or a different `:id`.

A live ID is unique within its instance and partition. A duplicate start returns the running PID and leaves that Agent unchanged.

```elixir
{:error, {:already_started, ^desk}} =
  Jido.start_agent(StartGuide.Jido, StartGuide.TicketDesk, id: "desk-1")
```

The worker links to its supervisor, so the Agent keeps running after the process that started it exits.

## Find a live Agent

Look up an Agent by ID when you do not hold its PID. These functions report only Agents that have finished startup.

```elixir
^desk = Jido.whereis_agent(StartGuide.Jido, "desk-1")
nil = Jido.whereis_agent(StartGuide.Jido, "desk-404")

ids = StartGuide.Jido |> Jido.list_agents() |> Enum.map(&elem(&1, 0)) |> Enum.sort()
{ids, Jido.resident_agent_count(StartGuide.Jido)}
# The cell returns {["desk-1", "desk-2"], 2}
```

## Use a partition

A partition is a nonempty string that gives one instance a separate ID space. The same ID in another partition is a different Agent. Lookups without `:partition` see only Agents with no partition.

```elixir
{:ok, acme_desk} =
  Jido.start_agent(StartGuide.Jido, StartGuide.TicketDesk, id: "desk-1", partition: "acme")

^acme_desk = Jido.whereis_agent(StartGuide.Jido, "desk-1", partition: "acme")
true = acme_desk != desk

{Jido.resident_agent_count(StartGuide.Jido), Jido.resident_agent_count(StartGuide.Jido, partition: "acme")}
# The cell returns {2, 1}
```

## Address an Agent by stable Address

A PID changes when a process restarts. A `Jido.Agent.Address` names the logical Agent with its namespace, partition, and ID. Store the Address in your application and let each operation resolve the current process.

```elixir
{:ok, address} = Jido.agent_address(StartGuide.Jido, "desk-3", partition: "acme")
{:ok, ^address} = Jido.create_agent(StartGuide.Jido, address, StartGuide.TicketDesk)
{:ok, desk_server} = Jido.activate_agent(StartGuide.Jido, address)
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(desk_server)

{:ok, ^desk_server} = Jido.resolve_agent(StartGuide.Jido, address)

{:ok, %Jido.AgentServer.TurnResult{status: :committed}} =
  Jido.call(StartGuide.Jido, address, StartGuide.TicketDesk.open_signal!(%{subject: "Printer jam"}))

%{opened: ["Printer jam"]} = Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(desk_server).agent.state

Jido.Agent.Address.to_map!(address)
# The cell returns %{"id" => "desk-3", "namespace" => "guides/support", "partition" => "acme", "version" => 1}
```

`Jido.Agent.Address.to_map!/1` and `Jido.Agent.Address.from_map/1` give you a form to save. A Address is an identifier, not a credential.

Kill the worker to see the Address follow the replacement process. The activation restarts it with the last committed state.

```elixir
monitor = Process.monitor(desk_server)
Process.exit(desk_server, :kill)

receive do
  {:DOWN, ^monitor, :process, ^desk_server, :killed} -> :ok
after
  1_000 -> raise "the worker did not exit"
end

replacement =
  Enum.find_value(1..100, fn _attempt ->
    Process.sleep(10)

    case Jido.resolve_agent(StartGuide.Jido, address) do
      {:ok, pid} when pid != desk_server -> pid
      _not_ready -> nil
    end
  end)

{replacement != desk_server, Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(replacement).agent.state}
# The cell returns {true, %{opened: ["Printer jam"]}}
```

Each Address call resolves once. It does not retry or replay a Turn. `Jido.resolve_agent/2` and the functions built on it return one of these errors when they cannot reach a worker:

| Error | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `{:error, :not_found}` | No live registration exists. |
| `{:error, :recovering}` | The worker is registered but not ready. |
| `{:error, :unavailable}` | The registry could not be read, or the worker exited during the call. |

Address functions need an instance with a `:namespace`. Without one, they return a `Jido.Error.ValidationError` with the code `:jido_namespace_required`.

## Give an Agent an OTP name

The `:name` option registers the worker under an OTP name: an atom, `{:global, term}`, or `{:via, module, term}`. Every `Jido.AgentServer` function accepts that name in place of a PID.

```elixir
{:ok, front_desk} =
  Jido.start_agent(StartGuide.Jido, StartGuide.TicketDesk, id: "front", name: StartGuide.FrontDesk)

^front_desk = Process.whereis(StartGuide.FrontDesk)
Process.alive?(front_desk)
# The cell returns true
```

Use a name only for a small, fixed set of Agents, because the runtime does not garbage collect atoms.

## Stop an Agent

`Jido.stop_agent/3` accepts a PID or an ID, and it confirms that the whole activation has exited. An ID in a partition needs the `:partition` option. `Jido.AgentServer.stop/3` stops a directly addressed activation and returns its final snapshot.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(StartGuide.Jido, "desk-1")
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(StartGuide.Jido, "desk-1", partition: "acme")
{:ok, _snapshot} = Jido.AgentServer.stop(replacement)

{Jido.stop_agent(StartGuide.Jido, "desk-1"), Jido.resolve_agent(StartGuide.Jido, address)}
# The cell returns {{:error, :not_found}, {:error, :not_found}}
```

`Jido.stop/1` stops the instance and every Agent in it.

